# Python Standards

Enforcement: **B**. Defer to in-repo tooling and style when present.

## Design

- Idiomatic Python (PEP 8 spirit); clarity over cleverness.
- Type hints on public functions and non-trivial internals; run the project’s checker (mypy/pyright) when configured.
- Clear package boundaries; avoid circular imports via structure, not hacks.
- Explicit exceptions; narrow `except`; use context managers for resources.

## Concurrency & I/O

- Use `asyncio` only when the stack is async; do not mix blocking I/O into async hot paths without executors.
- Prefer well-known HTTP/DB clients already in the project.
- For data workloads: vectorized/batch operations over Python loops on large frames when libraries are already used (pandas/polars/spark — do not invent a stack).

## APIs & data access

- Validate external input; separate domain logic from framework adapters.
- Parameterized SQL / ORM APIs only; never f-string SQL with user data.
- Manage sessions/connections explicitly; commit/rollback deliberately.

## Tooling (when available in repo or approved env)

| Concern | Common tools |
|---------|----------------|
| Lint/format | Ruff (or project formatter) |
| Types | mypy or Pyright |
| Tests | pytest, Coverage.py |
| Security | Bandit, pip-audit / OSV |

Run what exists; report what you could not run.

## Security & observability

- No secrets in code or logs.
- Structured logging; correlate requests where applicable.
- See `security.mdc`.

## Testing

- pytest idioms matching the repo.
- Parametrize edge cases; assert behavior, not private internals.
- Record command + exit code; never fabricate results.
